\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Setup}

We fine-tune Qwen-2.5-7B-Instruct with LoRA \citep{hu2022lora,dettmers2023qlora} on 8K multi-turn conversations from the UltraChat \citep{ding2023ultrachat} training split. Training uses 3 epochs with AdamW (lr $= 2 \times 10^{-4}$, batch size 1, gradient accumulation 8, seed 42; ThreadGate additionally with seed 123); each variant requires approximately 6 hours on a single NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell Server Edition GPU (96\,GB; training costs in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-cost}). Full hyperparameters are in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-hyper}; dataset statistics in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-data}; the controlled comparison design is described in \S\ref{sec:comparison-design}. We evaluate under \emph{IID} (same-distribution UltraChat text) and \emph{held-out} (50 non-overlapping conversations) teacher-forced protocols, reporting GAR-ours, GAR-Dongre \citep{dongre2026attention}, and PPL.

\subsection{Main Results}

Table~\ref{tab:four-way} presents the five-way comparison. Under TF evaluation, all fine-tuned variants converge: GAR-ours spans 0.49--0.51 ($\leq$2\,pp), PPL is 2.78 for all. IID and held-out protocols produce identical rankings (held-out GAR-ours differs by $\leq$1\,pp), ruling out data overlap as a confound. GAR-Dongre remains low (0.01--0.02) with no TF-AR gap, confirming that only concentrated last-token attention is vulnerable to distribution shift; scheduled sampling breaks this pattern (\S\ref{subsec:ss}).

Under AR generation ($N$\,=\,50), GAR-ours drops to 0.019--0.064, an 8--17$\times$ gap (Figure~\ref{fig:headline}). The ordering (ThreadGate 0.064 $>$ LoRA-only 0.037 $>$ Uniform Gate 0.033 $>$ PCGrad 0.030 $>$ Base 0.019) shows ThreadGate vs.\ LoRA-only is significant ($p$\,=\,0.004; Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-stats}), yet the gap remains 7.8$\times$. A second seed confirms TF stability ($0.504\pm0.001$). MMLU 5-shot accuracy remains within 0.8\,pp across all variants (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-mmlu}). We analyze this distribution shift in \S\ref{sec:analysis}.

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Five-way comparison on Qwen-2.5-7B-Instruct. TF evaluation uses IID UltraChat and 50 held-out conversations; AR evaluation uses $N$\,=\,50 held-out conversations (mean 3.6 user turns). All fine-tuned variants converge under TF ($\leq$2\,pp spread); the 8--17$\times$ TF-AR gap persists across all configurations. $\bar{g}\!\pm\!\sigma_g$: learned gate mean and std across 28 layers (LoRA-only has no gates). GAR-O = GAR-ours; GAR-D = GAR-Dongre \citep{dongre2026attention}.}
\label{tab:four-way}
\begin{tabular}{l ccc ccc cc c}
\toprule
& \multicolumn{3}{c}{\textbf{IID (TF)}} & \multicolumn{3}{c}{\textbf{Held-out (TF)}} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{AR ($N$\,=\,50)}} & \\
\cmidrule(lr){2-4} \cmidrule(lr){5-7} \cmidrule(lr){8-9}
\textbf{Configuration} & GAR-O & GAR-D & PPL & GAR-O & GAR-D & PPL & GAR-O & 95\% CI & $\bar{g}\!\pm\!\sigma_g$ \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{10}{l}{\emph{Baseline}} \\
Base              & 0.01 & 0.00 & 4.23 & 0.01 & 0.00 & 4.34 & 0.019 & {\scriptsize[.014,\,.024]} & --- \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{10}{l}{\emph{Gated variants}} \\
ThreadGate        & 0.50 & \textbf{0.02} & 2.78 & 0.50 & \textbf{0.02} & 2.78 & \textbf{0.064} & {\scriptsize[.041,\,.091]} & .93\,{\scriptsize$\pm$\,.05} \\
\;+\,PCGrad       & 0.50 & 0.01 & 2.78 & 0.50 & 0.01 & 2.78 & 0.030 & {\scriptsize[.023,\,.038]} & --- \\
Uniform Gate      & 0.49 & 0.01 & 2.78 & \textbf{0.51} & 0.01 & 2.78 & 0.033 & {\scriptsize[.025,\,.043]} & .96\,{\scriptsize$\pm$\,.05} \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{10}{l}{\emph{Ungated}} \\
LoRA-only         & 0.50 & 0.01 & 2.78 & 0.50 & 0.01 & 2.78 & 0.037 & {\scriptsize[.027,\,.050]} & 1.00 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table*}

\begin{figure*}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig4_attention_heatmap.pdf}
  \caption{Per-turn GAR-ours under AR generation ($N$\,=\,5 conversations, 10 turns). All fine-tuned variants cluster together (77--81\% decay; $\Delta$\,=\,3.3\,pp between ThreadGate and LoRA-only); Base decays 87.5\%. Gating adds zero incremental effect on the attention decay pattern.}
  \label{fig:attention-heatmap}
\end{figure*}

\subsection{Inference-Time Attention Steering}\label{subsec:steering}

All training-time interventions leave the exposure bias gap intact. If the collapse is caused by attention misallocation rather than representation degradation, then directly correcting attention should recover GAR. We test this by adding a constant bias $\beta$ to pre-softmax attention logits for goal-token positions during generation:
\begin{equation}
  \tilde{A}_{i,j}^{(\ell,h)} = A_{i,j}^{(\ell,h)} + \beta \cdot \mathbf{1}[j \in \mathcal{G}],
\end{equation}
where $\tilde{A}$ denotes the modified logits before softmax. The additive form operates in log-probability space, providing uniform boost even when baseline attention is near zero. The response is sharply nonlinear: $\beta \in [0, 2]$ yields marginal improvement, then $\beta = 3$ triggers a phase transition to GAR\,=\,0.556 (9\% PPL cost). At $\beta$\,=\,5, GAR reaches 0.727$\pm$0.061 (PPL 1.77); the operating region $\beta \in [3, 5]$ exceeds the TF baseline while PPL remains below 1.8 (Figure~\ref{fig:steering-recovery}; full results in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-steering}; per-$\beta$ turn-level profiles in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-steering-viz}).

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig5_attention_steering.pdf}
  \caption{Inference-time $\beta$-steering recovers GAR from 0.064 to 0.727, confirming attention misallocation as the proximal cause (9\% PPL cost at $\beta$\,=\,3; phase transition at $\beta \in [2, 3]$).}
  \label{fig:steering-recovery}
\end{figure}

This closes the causal loop: if the collapse were due to representation drift in hidden states, no amount of attention reweighting could recover GAR. The recovery confirms attention misallocation, not representation degradation, as the proximal cause (qualitative examples in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-examples}).

\subsection{Scheduled Sampling Compresses the TF--AR Gap}\label{subsec:ss}

Scheduled sampling \citep{bengio2015scheduled}, which mixes model-generated tokens into training prefixes with linearly increasing probability (implementation details in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-ss}), directly targets the training-inference mismatch identified in \S\ref{subsec:dist-shift}. Across three seeds, AR GAR-ours rises from 0.064 to $0.401\pm0.047$ (seeds: 0.347 / 0.423 / 0.433; per-seed details in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-perseed}), compressing the gap from 7.8$\times$ to ${\sim}$1.3$\times$ (per-seed gaps: 1.45$\times$, 1.17$\times$, 1.15$\times$; TF GAR $0.498\pm0.004$) with no PPL cost ($1.441\pm0.001$; Figure~\ref{fig:ss-gap}). Steering on SS models is remarkably stable ($\beta$\,=\,5: $0.717\pm0.001$): the training-time seed variance (std\,=\,0.047 at $\beta$\,=\,0) is nearly eliminated (Figure~\ref{fig:ss-multiseed}), suggesting $\beta$-steering converges to a seed-independent attention equilibrium. The improvement generalizes across GAR definitions: SS raises mid-turn GAR above 0.36 under all four alternative token-position variants (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-gar-robust}), while ThreadGate only improves last-token GAR (0.325 vs.\ all-token 0.025): SS builds a broad goal-attention pattern, not position-specific overfitting. GAR-Dongre also rises substantially under SS ($N$\,=\,5 pilot; see Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-n5}), the only intervention that moves both metrics. The benefit diminishes in longer dialogues (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-n5}), consistent with exposure bias compounding over turns.

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig_ss_gap_compression.pdf}
  \caption{Scheduled sampling compresses the TF-AR gap from 7.8$\times$ to ${\sim}$1.3$\times$, the only variant approaching parity across five training conditions.}
  \label{fig:ss-gap}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig_ss_multiseed.pdf}
  \caption{Multi-seed AR GAR under scheduled sampling ($\beta$\,=\,0 and $\beta$\,=\,5, three seeds). Steering nearly eliminates seed variance (std: 0.047$\to$0.001).}
  \label{fig:ss-multiseed}
\end{figure}

